<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Delian League</h1><p class="st-meta">478 BCE – 404 BCE · capital Athens</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Athenian-led alliance formed against Persia in 478 BCE that turned into an Athenian empire funding the Parthenon and Athens' war with Sparta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>, the Ionian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> wanted to continue the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and resented the Spartan commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a>. In 478-477 BCE they swore an alliance with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> on the sacred island of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delos">Delos</a>. The Athenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a> assessed each member's contribution of ships or money, set at 460 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> a year.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Delegates first met on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delos">Delos</a>, where the treasury was kept, but <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> controlled the fleet and the treasurers. Over time most members paid tribute rather than providing ships, and revolts by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a> (c. 470 BCE), <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thasos">Thasos</a> (465 BCE) and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/samos">Samos</a> (440 BCE) were crushed. The treasury moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 454 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> used its funds for the buildings of the Acropolis. Athens imposed its coinage, courts and settlers on allies.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon's</a> victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-eurymedon">Eurymedon</a> (c. 466 BCE) secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. Rivalry with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, in which many allies revolted after the Sicilian disaster of 413 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>' surrender in 404 BCE dissolved the league.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/delian-league.jpg" alt="Delian League" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Map_Peloponnesian_War_431_BC-fr.svg: Marsyas derivative work: Aeonx (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Peloponnesian_War_431_BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>478 BCE – 404 BCE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Athens</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aristides, Cimon, Pericles</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece, Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Athenian Empire, Athenian League</dd></dl></aside></div>
